<article class="wrap">
  <div class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/compare">Compare</a> › Seat Sherpa vs Uber and Lyft for long-distance trips</div>
  <h1>Seat Sherpa vs Uber and Lyft for long-distance trips</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated September 2026 · Prices are typical ranges; live seat prices are on each route page</div>

  <p class="lede"><strong>Short answer:</strong> Uber and Lyft are ride-hailing: a commercial driver is dispatched to you and paid per mile and per minute, which is why San Francisco to Los Angeles runs $300 to $500 or more, if a driver accepts it at all. Seat Sherpa is carpooling: a driver who is already making the trip posts it ahead of time and you share their cost, capped at the IRS mileage rate split across seats and never more than $55 a seat ($60 on trips over 400 miles).</p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table class="stack">
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Seat Sherpa</th><th>Uber / Lyft</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What it is</td><td class="win" data-l="Seat Sherpa">Scheduled carpool: book a seat on a trip the driver is taking anyway</td><td data-l="Uber / Lyft">On-demand ride-hailing inside metro areas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SF to LA, one person</td><td class="win" data-l="Seat Sherpa">$32 to $52.50 all in</td><td data-l="Uber / Lyft">$300 to $500+</td></tr>
      <tr><td>LA to Las Vegas, one person</td><td class="win" data-l="Seat Sherpa">$27 to $52.50 all in</td><td data-l="Uber / Lyft">$250 to $400+</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pricing</td><td class="win" data-l="Seat Sherpa">Capped at the IRS mileage rate divided across seats; no surge</td><td data-l="Uber / Lyft">Per mile + per minute, surge at peak times</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Booking</td><td data-l="Seat Sherpa">Book ahead, usually a day to a week out; instant-book or request</td><td class="win" data-l="Uber / Lyft">Minutes away, any time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Driver</td><td data-l="Seat Sherpa">Everyday driver: phone, license, vehicle and Stripe identity verified; public rating</td><td data-l="Uber / Lyft">Commercial TNC driver with background check</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Route</td><td data-l="Seat Sherpa">Fixed intercity route, exact pickup arranged in chat; some rides add stops along the way</td><td class="win" data-l="Uber / Lyft">Anywhere the app operates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payment</td><td class="win" data-l="Seat Sherpa">Held by Stripe until your pickup code is entered; refunded if the ride does not happen</td><td data-l="Uber / Lyft">Charged at trip end</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Insurance</td><td data-l="Seat Sherpa">Cost-sharing carpool, the driver’s personal policy applies</td><td class="win" data-l="Uber / Lyft">Commercial policy while on trip</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Why the price gap is so large</h2>
  <p>An Uber driver has to be paid for the 380 miles to Los Angeles and, in practice, for the empty 380 miles home. Uber’s per-mile intercity pricing reflects that, and drivers frequently cancel long requests because the deadhead return is not worth it. A Seat Sherpa driver was going to Los Angeles regardless. The passenger is not paying for a driver’s time; they are covering a share of gas and wear, and the app enforces the cap on every listing.</p>

  <h2>When Uber or Lyft is still the better choice</h2>
  <p>Inside a city, for a trip under about 30 miles, or when you need a car in the next 10 minutes. Seat Sherpa does not do on-demand pickups and does not operate within a single metro. Many riders combine the two: Uber to the pickup point, carpool for the 300 miles, Uber the last few miles at the other end.</p>

  <div class="callout" style="text-align:center">
    <h3>See live seat prices on your route</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom:6px">Drivers post trips they are already taking. Book in the browser or the app; no account needed to look.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/rides?c=compare">Browse rides →</a>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--cap);margin-top:10px">Driving somewhere yourself? <a href="/post">Post the ride</a> and split the cost.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq"><h3>How much is an Uber from San Francisco to Los Angeles?</h3><p>Typically $300 to $500 or more one way, before tip, and higher during surge. Many drivers decline a request that long. A Seat Sherpa carpool seat on the same route is capped at $55 a seat on the direct drive, $57.75 with the booking fee.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Can you take an Uber from LA to Las Vegas?</h3><p>Yes, if a driver accepts it, and it usually costs $250 to $400 or more one way. A carpool seat with Seat Sherpa on the LA to Las Vegas route is typically $27 to $52.50 with the booking fee.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is Seat Sherpa a rideshare company like Uber?</h3><p>No. Seat Sherpa is a carpool marketplace: drivers post trips they are already taking and passengers share the cost, capped at the IRS mileage rate. Drivers do not profit and there is no dispatch. Uber and Lyft are transportation network companies with commercial per-mile pricing.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>How much does a Seat Sherpa seat cost?</h3><p>It depends on the route and the driver, but every seat has a hard cap: the IRS standard mileage rate (about $0.76 per mile in 2026) multiplied by the drivable miles, divided across the seats, and never more than $55 a seat ($60 on trips over 400 miles). Riders pay the seat price plus a booking fee (5%, at least $2), and every price we show includes it. Live prices for each corridor are on the route pages and in the ride browser.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is Seat Sherpa safe?</h3><p>Every driver verifies a phone number by SMS, uploads a driver’s license that is checked for expiry, registers the vehicle, and connects a Stripe account that verifies identity and bank details. Riders see the driver’s photo, trip count and reviews before booking, can filter for women drivers, and can block anyone. A 4-digit pickup code confirms the rider got in the car and is what releases payment.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What is the cancellation policy?</h3><p>One standard policy on every ride: cancel 24 or more hours before departure for a full seat refund, 2 to 24 hours before for 50%, and inside 2 hours there is no refund. The booking fee is non-refundable when the rider cancels. If the driver cancels, the rider gets 100% back including the fee, and the same applies if the driver changes the departure time after the rider booked, up to 2 hours before the new departure.</p></div>

